{"id":"word-break","title":"Word Break","difficulty":"Medium","pattern":"1-D Dynamic Programming","tags":["String","Dynamic Programming","Hash Table","Trie"],"companies":{"google":3,"apple":3,"meta":4,"amazon":4,"microsoft":3},"description":"Given a string `s` and a list of dictionary words `wordDict`, decide whether `s` can be split into a sequence of one or more dictionary words placed end to end. Words may be reused any number of times.","examples":[{"input":"s = \"leetcode\", wordDict = [\"leet\",\"code\"]","output":"true"},{"input":"s = \"applepenapple\", wordDict = [\"apple\",\"pen\"]","output":"true","explanation":"\"apple\" is reused."},{"input":"s = \"catsandog\", wordDict = [\"cats\",\"dog\",\"sand\",\"and\",\"cat\"]","output":"false","explanation":"Every split leaves an unmatched piece (\"og\" or \"andog\")."}],"constraints":["1 <= s.length <= 300","1 <= wordDict.length <= 1000","1 <= wordDict[i].length <= 20","Lowercase English letters; dictionary words are distinct."],"hints":["Let ok[i] mean 's[:i] can be segmented'. ok[0] is true (empty prefix).","ok[i] is true if some j < i has ok[j] true and s[j:i] in the dictionary.","Only look back as far as the longest dictionary word, and store the dictionary in a set for O(1) membership."],"function":"wordBreak","signature":"def wordBreak(self, s: str, wordDict: List[str]) -> bool:","arg_types":{"s":"str","wordDict":"List[str]"},"return_type":"bool","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"s":"leetcode","wordDict":["leet","code"]},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"applepenapple","wordDict":["apple","pen"]},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"catsandog","wordDict":["cats","dog","sand","and","cat"]},"output":false},{"input":{"s":"a","wordDict":["b"]},"output":false},{"input":{"s":"cars","wordDict":["car","ca","rs"]},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"aaaaaaa","wordDict":["aaaa","aaa"]},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"","wordDict":["a","aa","aaa","aaaa"]},"output":false},{"input":{"s":"","wordDict":["cats","dog","sand","and","cat"]},"output":true}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def wordBreak(self, s: str, wordDict: List[str]) -> bool:\n        words = set(wordDict)\n        longest = max(len(w) for w in words)\n        ok = [True] + [False] * len(s)  # ok[i]: s[:i] is segmentable\n        for i in range(1, len(s) + 1):\n            for j in range(max(0, i - longest), i):\n                if ok[j] and s[j:i] in words:\n                    ok[i] = True\n                    break\n        return ok[-1]\n","approach":"**Whether a prefix is segmentable depends only on shorter prefixes: the last word must end at the prefix boundary.** Let `ok[i]` be true when `s[:i]` can be segmented. Then\n\n`ok[i] = any(ok[j] and s[j:i] in words for j in range(i))`, with `ok[0] = True`.\n\nFill left to right. Restricting `j` to the last `maxlen` positions is a free win because a longer slice can never be a dictionary word. The set makes membership O(1) (plus the O(L) hashing of the slice).\n\n**Why it works:** any valid segmentation of `s[:i]` ends with a word `s[j:i]`, and the part before it is a valid segmentation of `s[:j]`; conversely such a pair yields a segmentation of `s[:i]`. Memoised top-down recursion from index 0 is the same DP in reverse.\n\n**Common mistakes:** naive recursion without memoisation (exponential on inputs like 'aaaa...ab'), scanning the whole dictionary at every position instead of slicing, and treating `wordDict` as a list for membership (O(dictionary size) per check).\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Return all segmentations (Word Break II: memoised DFS returning lists; explain why the output can be exponential). Find a segmentation with the fewest words? (Store `best[i]` = min count, same transitions.) Speed up the inner loop with a trie walked from `j` forward, or when the dictionary is huge compared with `s`?","complexity":{"time":"O(n * L^2) where L is the longest word (O(n * L) comparisons each hashing up to L chars)","space":"O(n + total dictionary size)"},"similar":["word-break-ii","decode-ways","concatenated-words"],"number":132,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def wordBreak(self, s: str, wordDict: List[str]) -> bool:\n        pass\n"}